  // The cards that have left the set (cards.js RETIRED), with the words they had: a game saved with one in it still
  // shows it, and so does the history of an old game. Nothing else reads these.
  var RETIRED = {
    scheherazade: { cap: 'The Thousand and One Nights', ll: [44.36, 33.31], tx: 'She can\'t be clashed.', fact: 'In the old tale she saves her life by telling the king a story each night for 1,001 nights.', art: 'Shahrazad (Scheherazade), the learned vizier\'s daughter of the Thousand and One Nights, telling a tale by night, composed like a medieval Persian manuscript painting: a poised young woman in modest, finely woven court dress with long sleeves and a light headscarf, seated on a carpet in the lower part of the picture with open books beside her and her younger sister Dunyazad listening, one hand raised in mid-story. From her words a great swirling cloud of the tales themselves rises and fills the upper two thirds of the picture: a merchant ship in a storm, the flying ebony horse with its rider, and a towering djinn drawn the way djinn appear in medieval Arabic and Persian manuscripts such as the Book of Wonders: a horned, fanged, fierce-faced spirit of smokeless fire with clawed hands, a tail, and flame curling from its body. The cloud rises from her storytelling and the incense smoke of a small brazier, not from a lamp. Dignified, intelligent, fully and modestly clothed. No magic lamp, no muscular blue genie, no turbaned cartoon genie, no dancer costume, no bare shoulders.' },
    anansi: { cap: 'West African legend', ll: [-1.6, 6.7], tx: 'On arrival, gain 1 Spark, or 2 if the other player holds this Place.', fact: 'In Akan stories, the spider Anansi wins all the world\'s stories from the sky god by his wits.', art: 'Kwaku Anansi, the spider of the Akan stories of Ghana, drawn as a real spider with no human features at all: a huge orb-weaving spider seen dramatically from below, sitting at the very hub of a great orb web strung between giant forest trees. The web is built as a real web is built: straight spokes all radiating out from the hub where the spider sits, and spiral threads circling around it. The spider is drawn clearly and symmetrically like a diagram: exactly eight long legs and no other limbs of any kind, four legs on his left and four legs on his right, all eight fully visible from body to tip, spread wide apart like the spokes of a wheel with clear space between each, each foot resting on a thread of the web. None of his legs is curled, folded or hidden, and he has no short front arms, feelers or pedipalps. He is not holding anything. The small carved wooden box of all the world\'s stories hangs directly beneath his head, wrapped in bands of white silk and tied to the hub of the web by silk threads, as his prize. His body is tilted slightly to one side and his cluster of eight round black eyes catches the light like beads as it stares straight down at us, sly, watchful and pleased with himself. The markings on his abdomen fall in bold bands and diamonds, like the geometric patterns of Asante weaving. Green forest light breaks through the canopy behind him, with heavy black shadows. A true spider: no face, no mask, no human head, hands, hair or clothing; not gory. Compose it so the spider\'s body, eyes and the box are in the upper half of the picture and the top left corner is plain canopy.' },
    atalanta: { cap: 'Arcadia, Greek legend', ll: [22.3, 37.5], tx: 'Swift. She has +2 Might when she attacks a stronger Figure.', fact: 'In Greek myth she could outrun anyone and joined the hunt for the Calydonian boar.', art: 'Atalanta, a lean huntress in a short tunic, sprinting flat out through a forest with a bow in hand and hair flying, a great boar crashing through brush ahead.' },
    daedalus: { cap: 'Crete, Greek legend', ll: [25.16, 35.3], tx: 'Voyager: he can walk to any Place.', fact: 'In Greek myth he built the Labyrinth, then escaped Crete on wings of feathers and wax.', art: 'Daedalus, the master craftsman of Greek myth, at the moment before his escape from Crete: a wiry, grey-bearded craftsman in a short workman\'s tunic, standing at a cliff edge with both arms spread wide, wearing the wings he built: a great wing of real bird feathers bound with cord and wax strapped along each arm from shoulder to fingertip, like a bird about to launch into the wind. Far below him lies the vast stone Labyrinth and the sea of Bronze Age Crete. The wings are clearly worn on his two arms, one each side, not carried on his shoulder, and he holds nothing else.' },
    odysseus: { cap: 'Ithaca, Greek legend', ll: [20.7, 38.4], tx: 'Voyager. Steadfast: he has +2 Might when he defends.', fact: 'In Homer\'s Odyssey it takes him ten years to sail home from Troy.', art: 'Odysseus, weathered and cunning, gripping the steering oar of a small ship in heavy seas, a great bow slung on his back, a rocky island ahead.' },
    beowulf: { cap: 'Geatland, Old English legend', ll: [13.5, 58.0], tx: 'Guard. Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'The poem about him, over 3,000 lines long, survives in a single manuscript about a thousand years old.', art: 'Beowulf, a huge bearded warrior in a mail shirt and boar-crested helmet, standing guard in the doorway of a great timber hall at night, firelight behind, a monstrous shadow outside.' },
    trojan: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'A Figure costing 3 or less arrives from your hand at any Place.', fact: 'In the legend, Greek soldiers hid inside a giant wooden horse to get into Troy.', art: 'A giant wooden horse on wheels standing before open city gates at night, a hatch in its belly ajar with a rope dropping down.' },
    sinbad: { cap: 'The Thousand and One Nights', ll: [47.78, 30.5], tx: 'Voyager. On arrival, gain 1 Spark.', fact: 'In the tales he sails from Basra seven times, meets whales as big as islands and birds that carry off elephants, and comes home richer every time.', art: 'Sinbad the Sailor on his second voyage, in the tale from the Thousand and One Nights, escaping a desert island by tying himself to the leg of the Rukh, the giant bird: the picture is filled by the enormous bird in flight, seen from below and to one side, its wings spread across the whole upper half, each feather the length of a ship\'s oar, one huge scaled leg trailing. Lashed to that leg with the long unwound cloth of his own turban is Sinbad, a merchant of Abbasid Baghdad of about thirty with a short dark beard, bare-headed, in a long belted tunic and loose trousers, both arms wrapped around the leg, looking down wide-eyed. Far below are the sea, the small island with the white dome of the bird\'s egg on it, and his ship sailing away without him. Composed like a medieval Arabic manuscript painting of the Book of Wonders, with flat bright colour and patterned clouds. Sinbad and the leg are in the upper half, right of centre. A merchant of the ninth century: no bare chest and waistcoat, no curled slippers, no scimitar, no flying carpet, no genie. Keep the top left corner wing feathers and sky.' },
    gilgamesh: { cap: 'Uruk, Mesopotamian legend', ll: [45.64, 31.32], tx: 'Guard: enemies here must clash with him first.', fact: 'A real king of Uruk, some 4,700 years ago, probably stands behind the legend. The poem about him, written on clay tablets, is one of the oldest stories we have.', art: 'Gilgamesh, king of Uruk in the old Mesopotamian epic, standing on the top of the city wall he built, at sunrise, as the poem\'s first and last lines invite us to see it: a huge, broad-shouldered man drawn after the Sumerian statues of his own age, with long black hair parted in the middle and falling to his shoulders, a long squared beard cut in ridges, heavy brows and very wide eyes, bare to the waist, wearing a long skirt of tufted wool in rows, a plain band around his brow, and in one hand, resting on his shoulder, a copper axe of his age: a slender straight wooden haft with a small, narrow copper blade socketed near its end, a tool no bigger than a hatchet. He stands feet apart on the broad walkway of the wall in the right half of the picture, his free hand laid flat on the brickwork of the parapet. The wall of baked brick runs away from him in a long curve to the left with its buttresses and a gate, and inside it lie the flat-roofed city, date-palm gardens, clay pits and a white temple on a high terrace. Seen from a little below on the walkway, from the knees up. His head is a little right of the centre of the picture with a wide band of dawn sky above it: the top of his head is a fifth of the way down the picture, and the top right corner is empty sky. Early Sumer: no crown, no horned helmet, no winged bulls, no Assyrian royal dress, no lion, no writing, no broad double-curved battle axe. Keep the top left corner dawn sky.' },
  };
